/// Set whether the button participates in keyboard focus.
///
/// `ClickOnly` keeps the button hit-testable and pointer-activatable while
/// excluding it from keyboard traversal and keyboard activation. `Disabled`
/// suppresses keyboard and pointer activation and renders the disabled state.

/// Registers this Form button's keyboard stop without a pointer region.
///
/// Use only when Form layout clips the native button body. Disabled and
/// ClickOnly policy remains identical to the native draw path.

/// Registers this Form checkbox's keyboard stop without a pointer region.
///
/// Use only when Form layout clips the native checkbox body. Its native
/// disabled and read-only policy remains authoritative.

/// Registers this Form toggle's keyboard stop without a pointer region.
///
/// Use only when Form layout clips the native toggle body. Its native
/// disabled and read-only policy remains authoritative.

/// Registers this Form radio group's keyboard stop without a pointer region.
///
/// Form calls this only when at least one original option remains but its
/// native option window is clipped. Empty groups remain noninteractive.

/// Keep this exact master-pane width until the split is manually resized.
/// Configured minima can clamp the extent. `ResetPolicy::Initial` restores
/// the cell width, and a later `master_share` call replaces this policy.
/// This selects the resizable sizing mode; a later fixed-share builder can
/// still select fixed sizing. Other builders and the legacy default keep
/// their existing percentage.

/// Use a fixed share for the detail pane, measured from the full width.
/// The requested width is floored and clamped to `min..=max`. Calling
/// [`Self::resizable`] later restores the stored interactive split ratio.

/// Use a fixed share for the master pane, measured from the full width.
/// The requested width is rounded to the nearest cell and clamped to
/// `min..=max`. Calling [`Self::resizable`] later restores the stored
/// interactive split ratio.

/// Return to an interactive split after a fixed-share builder.
///
/// The most recent sizing-mode builder wins. This does not change the
/// stateful ratio configured by [`Self::master_share`] or retained in
/// [`MasterDetailState`].

/// Preserve the pointer's grab offset while dragging narrow aligned seams.
///
/// The default remains the historical raw-coordinate behavior. When
/// enabled, the seam follows the pointer by the same number of cells.

/// Set the static resize command table used both for hints and handling.
/// An empty table disables keyboard resizing while leaving seam dragging
/// available in resizable mode.

/// Set the destination used when the split seam is reset.
///
/// The default remains balanced to preserve the existing control contract.

/// Resolve shared responsive mode and narrow summary geometry.
///
/// Split pane geometry remains owned by the configured [`SplitPane`]. In
/// split mode, the pane rectangles in the returned value are empty; use
/// [`Self::layout`] when this control's split regions are needed.

/// Draw the panel rows visible through a validated window into its original
/// logical area.
///
/// The callback receives the visible portion of the panel's original inner
/// body, rebased to that body's source rows. A partial window cannot replay
/// arbitrary legacy slots; it returns an error before painting, registering
/// decoration, or invoking the callback. Full windows preserve [`Self::draw`]
/// behavior.

/// Measure this list from its live rows and keyed state. The same row
/// geometry used by update and draw determines wrapping and copy-hint
/// reservation; `measure` remains available for legacy callers.

/// Precise ratio retained after a manual resize.
///
/// An opted-in initial-cell split uses its configured cell extent until
/// manual input changes this value; query the resolved pane for that width.

/// Preserve the pointer's grab offset when dragging a narrow aligned seam.
///
/// By default, narrow seam drags continue to use the pointer's raw
/// coordinate as the first-pane edge. Enable this policy when the seam
/// should move by the same number of cells as the pointer.

/// Replace `replace_len` bytes immediately before the cursor with a completion.
///
/// The editor snaps the replacement range to its existing grapheme boundaries,
/// then applies the edit through the normal generation, find, and scroll path.

/// Commit the edit session when focus leaves this editor.
///
/// Defaults to `true`. Set to `false` when the owning view temporarily removes the editor
/// while preserving its edit session, such as switching to another document tab. Explicit
/// cancel and editor commands that leave focus continue to commit independently.

/// Open the find bar and focus this editor.
///
/// Find remains available for read-only editors. Disabled editors ignore the request.

/// Return the currently selected value while its completion popup is open.
///
/// Selection resolves through the collection's stable key, so a stale
/// cursor or a closed popup never exposes an unrelated vector entry.

/// Configure whether leaving the editor commits its edit session.
///
/// The default is to commit. Set to `false` when the owning view temporarily removes the
/// editor while preserving its edit session. Completion popups still dismiss on focus loss.

/// Explicit rectangles for an Accounts drawer composed around a live split.
///
/// `master_box` and `detail_box` exclude the shared page header and footer.
/// `master_rows` is the physical row window inside the master box. The split
/// control owns `seam`; the drawer painter leaves its live seam feedback
/// visible. `detail_content` is the unscrolled line-layout origin, while
/// `detail_body` is the logical scroll viewport and `detail_clip` is the
/// physical content window after scrollbar allocation. The title and fixed
/// action row remain outside that window.

/// Paint the shared Accounts shell using caller-owned split and body geometry.
///
/// Unlike [`paint_accounts_drawer`], this entrypoint does not choose pane
/// widths or paint scrolling body rows. It paints the same page header, master
/// frame/rows, drawer title/rule/action row, and footer using explicit
/// rectangles. The caller uses the window painters for `detail_body` and owns
/// its scrollbar and input state. The existing whole-page entrypoint retains
/// its fixed39-column behavior.

/// Paint borrowed account-drawer rows through a bounded physical window.
///
/// `content_layout` describes the full, unscrolled content column: its left
/// edge is the text origin and its right edge retains all right-aligned
/// metadata anchors. `content_clip` is a physical sub-rectangle of that
/// column, and `first_source_row` is the source row at its top edge. The
/// returned range contains the source rows visible through both the requested
/// clip and the current `Ui` clip. Empty clips are valid and return an empty
/// range. Invalid geometry or a source offset past `lines.len()` returns
/// `None` before painting any cells.
///
/// This painter leaves row backgrounds to its caller. Graphemes cut by a
/// horizontal clip edge are omitted whole; remaining text keeps its original
/// logical columns. The existing whole drawer painter keeps its historic
/// clipping behavior.

/// Project one typed drawer row into its full logical display text and styles.
///
/// This uses the same row-emission path as the existing painters. It does not
/// apply a physical clip: callers receive the complete logical row and can
/// retain the original [`DrawerLine`] for width-dependent rebuilding. Some
/// painter operations cannot be represented as one grapheme-safe row; those
/// inputs return a [`DrawerProjectionError`] instead of silently losing text
/// or style ownership.

/// Explicit wrapping widths for an Inspector visual-row measurement or window.
///
/// `text_width` is the width used by ordinary body rows and the second pass of
/// [`InspectLine::Prewrapped`]. `prewrap_width` is the first pass for that
/// variant. Callers choose both values from their resolved content geometry;
/// the Inspector does not infer a scrollbar gutter or a page layout here.

/// Count the wrapped visual rows the Inspector window painter will consume.
///
/// This uses the same typed line layout as the whole-body painter. The caller
/// supplies the resolved logical text and prewrap widths and should use this
/// count when preparing its own `ScrollState`.

/// Paint a vertical window of wrapped Inspector rows inside an existing pane.
///
/// `logical` is the full physical content viewport, `clip` is a vertically
/// clipped region within it, and `first_visual_row` is an offset into the
/// already wrapped visual rows (not an `InspectLine` index). Horizontal
/// anchoring stays at `logical.x`; a clip may reduce the right edge but may not
/// shift the text origin. The caller owns frames, backgrounds, scrollbars,
/// fades, input registration and scroll state.
///
/// Returns the source visual-row range painted after both the requested clip
/// and the surrounding [`Ui`] clip are applied. Invalid or empty requested
/// rectangles, widths that do not fit the logical viewport, an uncontained
/// requested clip, or an offset past the measured rows return `None` without
/// painting. If valid geometry is fully hidden by an ancestor clip, returns an
/// empty range at `first_visual_row` without painting.

/// Visit each logically cut row without allocating wrapped text.
///
/// `width` and `logical_height` must match the logical geometry supplied
/// to [`Self::draw`]. For [`Self::draw_window`], pass the visible body's
/// width and the window's logical row count. A physical viewport crop does
/// not create a cut; callers can map each returned row through their
/// validated window and clip.
///
/// The visitor receives rows in Notice order. Its row number is relative
/// to the complete Notice, and its text is the complete original source,
/// not the wrapped or painted row. The visible `mark_cut` setting does not
/// affect this metadata.

/// One safe value presentation for the live workspace Editor.
///
/// Plain values cross this boundary only as a star count. OnePassword paths
/// and HostEnv names are public display references, never resolved secrets.

/// Create the headings and visible role rows shown by the live Editor.
///
/// `rows` contains at most the 29 visible roles. `total_roles` preserves
/// the source-list size used by the overflow treatment.

/// Paint an Env body with the existing page chrome.
///
/// `page.sections`, `page.mounts`, and `page.add_mount` must be empty. The
/// function borrows all rows and paints every row or returns an error before
/// drawing. Horizontal text clips to the supplied area. Content taller than
/// the available body space is rejected; this entry point does not scroll or
/// handle input and focus.

/// Paint only the fixed environment body used by the live workspace Editor.
///
/// `area` is the full screen allocation. The body starts at row six and ends
/// before the fixed row-37 footer. This entry does not paint Editor chrome,
/// footer labels, or input controls. Plain values enter only as a star count.
/// The function validates the full current allocation and all body rows before
/// painting any ink. It does not scroll or drop rows. Each fixed key, value,
/// and source column clips its text to that column's terminal-cell width.
///
/// # Errors
///
/// Returns an error when the area is narrower than the fixed Editor columns,
/// shorter than the frame and footer, or cannot contain all body rows.

/// Paint only the fixed role list used by the live workspace Editor.
///
/// `area` is the full screen allocation. The headings start at row six and
/// the visible rows end before the fixed row-37 footer. This entry does not
/// paint Editor chrome, footer labels, or input controls. It supports at most
/// 29 visible rows and validates all borrowed content before painting. The
/// fixed columns accept printable single-cell text so callers can retain the
/// legacy painter for content outside this proven layout.
///
/// # Errors
///
/// Returns an error when the allocation is too small, the visible row count
/// is invalid, or any display string exceeds the fixed columns.

/// Paint an Agents table with the existing Settings page chrome.
///
/// `page.sections`, `page.mounts`, and `page.add_mount` must be empty. The
/// renderer supports at most six rows and two fixed notes. It validates the
/// complete layout before it paints any cell. This function does not handle
/// input or focus.

/// Paint only the fixed Agents content band within a Settings page area.
///
/// The band is page-relative rows 6 through 16, clipped to both `area` and the
/// current Ui clip. Unlike the full-page painter, this entrypoint does not draw
/// chrome or impose a minimum page height. Row and note capacities are checked
/// before any cell is changed.

/// Paint only the fixed Trust content band within a Settings page area.
///
/// The band is page-relative rows 6 through 13, clipped to both `area` and
/// the current Ui clip. Unlike the full-page painter, this entrypoint does
/// not draw chrome or impose a minimum page height. Row and note capacities
/// are checked before any cell is changed. This function does not handle
/// input, counts, or a caller-owned save preview.
///
/// # Errors
///
/// Returns [`SettingsBodyPaintError::TooManyRows`] or
/// [`SettingsBodyPaintError::TooManyNotes`] when the input exceeds the fixed
/// Trust body capacities. Either error is returned before painting.

/// Paint only the borrowed Mounts table within a Settings page area.
///
/// The table starts at page-relative row 6 and is clipped to the supplied
/// page area, the available body band above the native footer, and the current
/// Ui clip. It paints the shared Mounts columns and rows without page chrome,
/// actions, hints, input handling, or focus ownership.

/// Paint a Trust table with the existing Settings page chrome.
///
/// `page.sections`, `page.mounts`, and `page.add_mount` must be empty. The
/// renderer supports at most four rows and two fixed notes. It validates the
/// complete layout before it paints any cell. This function does not handle
/// input, counts, or a caller-owned save preview.

/// Stage and acknowledge the exact layer opening that temporarily moves focus
/// away from a field. The field state is updated before the runtime records the
/// acknowledgement.

/// Initial selection policy when an idle text input first receives focus.
///
/// Pointer focus leaves placement and selection to the pointer gesture. A draft
/// retained across a blur also keeps its existing caret and selection.

/// Leave selected editing commands available to a containing component.
///
/// The editor still owns rendering and state; excluded commands are simply
/// omitted from this input's binding table so the owner can route them.

/// Registers this Form input's keyboard stop without a pointer region.
///
/// Use only when Form layout clips the native input body. The native
/// disabled and read-only policy is preserved, including inherited Form
/// disabling.

/// Register this input's focus, pointer, and key-binding contract when a
/// host draws its own visible surface for the input state. The supplied
/// area must belong to the visible host control (for example, a searchable
/// list), not to an off-screen or hidden input. Unlike `draw`, this method
/// keeps the entire supplied host region for focus and pointer routing.

/// Geometry shared by TextArea drawing and coordinate-bearing input.
///
/// `window` describes the component body's visible rows. `text` is the
/// registered text part inside that body, so pointer, cursor and menu mapping
/// use the same physical origin and clipping bounds.

/// Project an active captured drag through the parent window. Unlike a
/// press, a captured drag remains meaningful after leaving `TEXT`; the
/// old logical child behavior extended to the document edge from the
/// captured origin. Clamp above the visible window to its first row, but
/// preserve vertical extrapolation below it so the caller can clamp to the
/// document edge rather than the parent's logical viewport.

/// Registers this Form text area's keyboard stop without a pointer region.
///
/// Use only when Form layout clips the native text area body. The native
/// disabled and read-only policy is preserved, including inherited Form
/// disabling.

/// Return the focused caret's source row inside this TextArea's logical
/// visible body, after the document's own scroll offset.
///
/// A containing Form can use this row to reveal the caret in its parent
/// viewport without reading or changing the document state.

/// Apply a borrowed semantic lane layout after checking its static shape
/// and field references. Active-group/data visibility completeness is
/// checked by [`Self::validate_layout`] before each rendering phase.

/// Keeps automatic reveal from moving a child hit target between pointer
/// press and release. The pointer-down intent starts the hold; release,
/// drag-end and cancellation end it. The snapshot's pressed flag is only
/// used to detect a gesture that became inactive without a terminal intent.

/// Estimates the legacy linear layout without consulting [`FormData`].
///
/// This compatibility method cannot account for data-dependent visibility,
/// radio option counts, or the row pairing used when `columns(2)` is set.
/// This estimate also ignores an explicit lane layout. Consumers with
/// form data or [`FieldSizingPolicy::Intrinsic`] should use
/// [`Self::measure_with_data`].

/// Measures the visible fields using the same placement plan as update and draw.
///
/// Measurement reads visibility and option data only. It does not update
/// controls, validate values, invoke domain handlers, or mutate form state.

/// Measures with explicit design tokens so composed overlays can use the
/// same placement policy before a [`Ui`] draw pass exists.
///
/// This is crate-visible; consumers should use [`Self::measure_with_data`].

/// Sets how Enter behaves when a form field is not editing.
///
/// The default submits, matching [`Form`]. Use [`EnterPolicy::Never`] when
/// the caller handles submission through a separate scoped command.

/// Submits the current native form values after a scoped command bubbles
/// past the focused child control.
///
/// The caller should run [`Self::update`] first so native controls can
/// consume the same input frame. Submission then commits any active draft,
/// validates through [`Form`], and applies this dialog's submit policy.

/// The column chrome a grid body resolved.
///
/// The scroll bars align to the grid body's gutter and content width, so the
/// host hands the resolved chrome to the bars instead of recomputing the
/// geometry from the column widths.

/// Registers this Form chip bar's keyboard stop without a pointer region.
///
/// Use only when Form layout clips the native chip bar body. Its native
/// disabled and read-only policy remains authoritative.

/// Handle an isolated focus loss without reconciling the list's source or layout.
///
/// `None` means the update cause is not `Settle`, or this owner has another
/// intent. Inspecting the owner bucket acknowledges it for diagnostics; any
/// unclaimed intents remain available to the ordinary update loop. Call
/// [`Self::update`] on this same list and state in the same pass after `None`.
/// An empty bucket returns `Some(Response::ignored())`.

/// Bindings for additive multi-selection commands such as SelectAll.
///
/// Single and non-selectable lists return an empty table. Multi and range
/// lists return their static override when one was configured, or the
/// shared default table otherwise. An explicitly empty override disables
/// these additional commands. Callers that publish the table must apply
/// the same non-inert, non-empty, and non-disabled guards as [`List::draw`].

/// Acknowledge an isolated focus loss without reconciling source or layout.
///
/// `NavListState` has no focus-loss state to update. `None` means the update
/// cause is not `Settle`, or this owner has another intent. Inspecting the
/// owner bucket acknowledges it for diagnostics; any unclaimed intents remain
/// available to the ordinary update loop. Call [`Self::update`] on this same
/// list, state, and pass after `None`. An empty bucket returns
/// `Some(Response::ignored())`.

/// Supply the terminal-cell width of the label produced by a custom row.
///
/// Measurement cannot invoke the row renderer because it paints into the
/// terminal. Custom rows that need intrinsic sizing beyond the 12-cell
/// label minimum can provide a pure data projection here. The projection
/// must describe the label actually rendered by the row callback.

/// Measure this rail from its borrowed items without updating component state.
///
/// Labels use the optional pure width projection first, then the row's
/// intrinsic label width when available, and otherwise a 12-cell minimum.
/// Measurement does not call the row painter or application state
/// projection.

/// Configuration errors for Tabs navigation and replacement New/Close groups.
///
/// A group may contain only its corresponding command. Action identities must
/// be unique across the effective Tabs table, and declared chords must not
/// collide under [`Chord::effective_eq`]. The default New or Close group is
/// omitted from validation when that same group is replaced.

/// Committed viewport counts supplied to a pure tabs layout pass.
///
/// This receipt is explicit because planning must not inspect a previously
/// painted frame. Pass the receipt returned by the preceding committed layout
/// when preserving the warm-frame overflow reservation rule.

/// Allocated close-target geometry for an eligible item. An explicitly
/// widened target may overlap label or mark cells. In
/// [`TabCloseReveal::Hover`] mode this remains present while the affordance
/// is hidden.

/// Clipped allocation for an eligible close target, not a promise that
/// the current frame registered a live hit. The committed UI hit registry
/// is authoritative for current interaction visibility.

/// Pure layout result for default display rows.
///
/// Layout owns every formatted default-row segment and all tab allocations.
/// Drawing a layout never invokes item Display or mark callbacks and never
/// scans rendered buffer cells to recover label extents.

/// Return logical and clipped allocated bounds for a tabs part.
///
/// A close bound is potential geometry for an eligible item. With
/// [`TabCloseReveal::Hover`], the close affordance may be hidden and have
/// no live hit target; query the committed UI hit registry to determine
/// current interaction visibility.

/// Select an adjacent-tab navigation profile.
///
/// `None` preserves the current default table exactly. A profile changes
/// only Prev/Next descriptors; Activate, digit, New, Close, pointer,
/// overflow, layout, and wrapping semantics stay unchanged. The selected
/// descriptors are used for both binding publication and dispatch.
///
/// # Errors
///
/// Returns an error when a current custom New or Close binding reuses an
/// action identity or effective chord with the selected profile or another
/// effective binding.

/// Replace the keyboard bindings for [`TabsCmd::New`].
///
/// The default `None` keeps the built-in New binding in the base
/// [`Bindings`] table. `Some(&[])` explicitly removes New keyboard
/// bindings while leaving the pointer New part governed by
/// [`Self::allow_new`]. A custom static table is published with its
/// original action, chord, label, priority, and visibility metadata.
///
/// The complete potential command table is validated, even when New is
/// currently disabled or this Tabs instance is passive. Use
/// [`Self::new_key_table`] to publish the supplemental table when painting
/// manually alongside [`Bindings::bindings`].
///
/// # Errors
///
/// Returns an error when a binding uses a command other than
/// [`TabsCmd::New`], reuses an effective action identity, or has a chord
/// that conflicts with another effective binding.

/// Replace the keyboard bindings for [`TabsCmd::Close`].
///
/// The default `None` keeps the built-in Close bindings in the base
/// [`Bindings`] table. `Some(&[])` explicitly removes Close keyboard
/// bindings without changing close-target eligibility, pointer clicks, or
/// Middle behavior. A custom static table is published with its original
/// action, chord, label, priority, and visibility metadata.
///
/// The complete potential command table is validated, even when no item
/// is currently closable or this Tabs instance is passive. Use
/// [`Self::close_key_table`] to publish the supplemental table when
/// painting manually alongside [`Bindings::bindings`].
///
/// # Errors
///
/// Returns an error when a binding uses a command other than
/// [`TabsCmd::Close`], reuses an effective action identity, or has a chord
/// that conflicts with another effective binding.

/// Choose when an eligible close affordance is visible and interactive.
///
/// The default is [`TabCloseReveal::Always`]. [`TabCloseReveal::Hover`]
/// keeps each eligible close target reserved so tab geometry does not
/// shift, but registers it only while its tab is active or focused, its
/// tab or close part is hovered, or its close part is pressed.

/// Widen the close hit target around its existing close glyph.
///
/// The default is `None`, which preserves the current one-cell target.
/// Explicit widths follow the centered one-cell-glyph plate calculation:
/// even requests round down to the nearest odd width, and zero means one
/// cell. The target is clamped within each tab and does not change tab
/// sizing or the reserved close-label space.

/// Override native-layer coverage for the dynamic active-rule policies.
///
/// Without this setting, [`TabRule::Auto`] and [`TabRule::FocusOnly`]
/// consult the current frame's native layer stack. Set this to `true` when
/// a host-owned overlay or popup hides the Tabs context without opening a
/// native layer; set it to `false` to explicitly present the rule as
/// uncovered. This changes only active-rule presentation, never focus,
/// hit registration, or input eligibility. It has no effect on the fixed
/// [`TabRule::Accent`] and [`TabRule::Neutral`] policies.

/// Keep the tab strip out of the automatic focus order and keyboard map.
///
/// Visible tab, close, new and overflow parts remain interactive. The
/// default is `false`, which preserves the ordinary focusable strip.

/// Choose whether a passive strip presents its cursor row as focused.
///
/// This only affects presentation while [`Self::passive`] is `true`; it
/// never grants focus or keyboard input. The default is `false`.

/// Map each item to independent optional status marks.
///
/// The default is unconfigured and does not reserve per-item space. A
/// configured mapper that returns clean marks also reserves no space.
/// Nonempty marks reserve two cells after the row content and before an
/// eligible close affordance. The mapper's result is copied into a
/// [`TabLayout`] when planning; rebuild that plan after changing item data
/// or the mapper to display the new facts.

/// Supplemental New bindings for the current keyboard configuration.
///
/// The built-in New command remains in the unchanged base table returned
/// by [`Bindings::bindings`] while no custom New table is configured. This
/// accessor returns only the extra table to publish when a custom table
/// replaces that default, and returns an empty slice when New is gated or
/// the Tabs owner is passive.

/// Supplemental Close bindings for the current keyboard configuration.
///
/// The built-in Close commands remain in the unchanged base table
/// returned by [`Bindings::bindings`] while no custom Close table is
/// configured. This accessor returns only the extra table to publish when
/// a custom table replaces those defaults, and returns an empty slice when
/// Close is gated or the Tabs owner is passive.

/// Map each item to optional prefix and suffix text for fresh planned
/// default-row layouts.
///
/// The mapper runs once for every item in each nonempty fresh plan,
/// including items outside the visible window. Its owned text is captured
/// with the original Display segments and reused for row geometry,
/// rendering, and the hidden-tabs menu. Empty-area plans do not invoke it.
/// The legacy custom-row renderer is unchanged.

/// Resolve default display rows without painting or reading a buffer.
///
/// Every fresh nonempty plan formats each item once and captures its key,
/// visible mark, close eligibility, and original Display write segments,
/// including items that are not allocated in the visible window. Rebuild
/// after item content changes; there is no persistent same-key text cache.
/// Empty-area plans preserve the original callback/key lookup behavior.
///
/// `previous` is the caller-owned receipt from the last committed draw.
/// It preserves the existing overflow reservation behavior across cold
/// and warm frames while keeping measurement independent of `Ui`.

/// Draw a previously resolved default-row layout.
///
/// Rebuild the plan after any state or data change. Returns `Err` when
/// component identity or rendering configuration differs from planning.

/// Set the right-side cell budget used when a row has a trailing mark.
///
/// The budget includes the mark and its following row-fill cells. The
/// default is two: the mark is painted at `row.right() - 2`, leaving one
/// cell after it. A value of three paints at `row.right() - 3`, leaving
/// two cells after it. Values below two are normalized to two.
///
/// This only narrows the default [`RowUi`] content lane for rows whose
/// [`Tree::trailing_mark`] callback returns a mark. A custom full-row
/// [`Tree::render_row`] renderer retains its full row and is not narrowed.

/// Accept an item through a caller-owned editor splice operation.
///
/// The completion controller still owns dismissal; the adapter owns how the
/// editor applies the byte range and insertion. This keeps editor state and
/// selection lifecycle in the editor crate without adding a dependency here.

/// Draw this dialog through its existing body layout and expose the body
/// as a typed component slot.
///
/// The body closure runs once, including when the resolved body has zero
/// area. Dialog chrome, focus registration, clipping, and hit regions
/// remain owned by [`Dialog::draw`].

/// Anchors this menu to a part registered by another rendered component.
/// Use [`Self::try_layer`] when opening it; that method returns `None` when
/// the owner or part is absent from the current frame.

/// Builds a layer only when its configured owner part is present.
/// Fixed point and rectangle anchors retain the compatibility behavior of
/// [`Self::layer`].

/// A passive, same-layer reveal for one caller-owned Notice.
///
/// `owner` must be stable and unique for the live Notice. Increment the
/// revision passed to [`Self::update`] and [`Self::draw`] whenever its text or
/// cut-related options change. The cut identity combines the semantic source
/// and Notice-relative row, so pointer movement within one row keeps the
/// original rest deadline while another row starts a new one.
///
/// This painter does not take focus, set a cursor, or handle pointer presses.
/// Its scroll area is passive: only vertical wheel input inside an overflowing
/// reveal is considered, and the runtime forwards input at its ends to another
/// scroll region on the same layer.

/// Observe the current passive hover and advance the 300 ms rest delay.
///
/// Call this from the owning app's update path, including tick and settle
/// updates. It stores only a stable part identity, revision, and time. The
/// current borrowed Notice is resolved by [`Self::draw`] each frame.

/// Clear the active reveal when its owning page or Notice becomes inactive.
///
/// A previously requested repaint deadline is harmless after this call:
/// `update` starts a fresh rest interval only from a currently registered
/// source row, and `draw` resolves that row against the current Notice.

/// Register current cut rows and paint the matching reveal after the
/// Notice's normal draw.
///
/// `logical_area` describes the un-clipped Notice body: its x, width, and
/// height must match the row-only `window`'s horizontal geometry and
/// logical row count. `window` supplies physical rows after scrolling;
/// the current Ui clip supplies the final visible intersection. Calling
/// this once after all live Notice content is drawn keeps the reveal above
/// the page while it stays in the current layer.
///
/// The returned rectangle is the actual painted pane, or `None` when the
/// current source is absent, off-window, clipped out, suppressed, or not
/// yet ready. The pane itself is paint-only apart from its opt-in passive
/// scroll region.

/// Geometry policy for a picker card.
///
/// [`PickerLayout::default`] preserves the original picker sizing and drawing
/// rules. [`PickerLayout::card`] provides a centered-card policy with explicit
/// viewport margins and content spacing. Apply `Picker::layout` before
